WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.620
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:10.170
This work doesn't have a ton of viewable footage,
but I think it is an absolutely amazing piece

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:14.980
of craftsmanship, so in the first half of
this video, we'll roll some footage from earlier

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:18.340
episodes, and in the second half, you'll see
the new stuff.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:22.609
In this series, we often talk about reinforcement
learning, which is a learning technique where

00:00:22.609 --> 00:00:28.220
an agent chooses an optimal series of actions
in an environment to maximize a score.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:33.410
Playing computer games is a good example of
a clearly defined score that is to be maximized.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:37.910
As long as we can say that the higher the
score, the better the learning, the concept

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:43.360
will work for helicopter control, choosing
the best spot for wifi connectivity or a large

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.309
variety of different tasks.

00:00:45.309 --> 00:00:50.539
However, what about environments where multiple
agents or players are present?

00:00:50.539 --> 00:00:55.270
Not all games are single player focused, and
not all helicopters have to fly alone.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:56.420
So what about that?

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:01.960
To deal with cases like this, scientists at
OpenAI and the University of Oxford came up

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:08.960
with a work by the name "Learning with Opponent-Learning
Awareness", LOLA in short, or lola.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.510
I have to say that the naming game at OpenAI
has been quite strong lately.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.900
This is about multiplayer reinforcement learning,
if you will.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:22.830
This new agent does not only care about maximizing
its own score but also inserts a new term

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:28.110
into the equation which is about anticipating
the actions of other players in the environment.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:33.110
It is not only possible to do this, but they
also show that it can be done in an effective

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:39.090
way, and, the best part is that it also gives
rise to classical strategies that game theory

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.920
practitioners will immediately recognize.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:47.030
For instance, it can learn tit for tat, which
is a strategy that mirrors the other player's

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:48.030
actions.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:52.460
This means that if the other player is cooperative,
it will remain cooperative, but if it gets

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:55.910
screwed over, it will also try to screw others
over.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:58.530
You'll see in a moment why this is a big deal.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:03.280
The prisoner's dilemma is a game where two
criminals are caught and are independently

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:07.610
interrogated, and have to choose whether they
snitch on the other one or not.

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:10.990
If any one snitches out, there will be hell
to pay for the other one.

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:15.090
If they both defect, they both serve a fair
amount time in prison.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:18.660
The score to be minimized is therefore this
time spent in prison.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:19.770
The optimal solution of this game is when
both criminals remain silent, and this strategy

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.530
is something that we call the Nash equilibrium.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:27.120
In other words, this is the best set of actions
if we consider the options of the other actor

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:34.670
as well and expect that they do the same for
us.

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:39.480
And now, the first cool result is that if
we run the prisoner's dilemma with two of

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:43.560
these new LOLA agents, they quickly find the
Nash equilibrium.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:44.560
This is great.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:48.530
But wait, we have talked about this tit for
tat thing, so what's the big deal with that?

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:53.810
There is an iterated version of the prisoner's
dilemma game, where this snitching or cooperating

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:56.300
game is replayed many many times.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:01.790
It is an ideal benchmark because an advanced
agent would know that we cooperated the last

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:05.910
time, so it is likely that we can partner
up this time around too!

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:07.780
And now comes the even cooler thing!

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:13.200
This is where the tit for tat strategy emerges
- these LOLA agents know that if the previous

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:18.640
time, they cooperated, they will immediately
give each other another chance, and again,

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.060
get away with the least amount of prison time.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:26.340
As you can see here, the results vastly outperform
other naive agents, and from the scores it

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:31.940
seems that previous techniques enter a snitching
revenge war against each other and both will

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:34.640
serve plenty of time in prison.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:40.020
Other games are also benchmarked against naive,
uncooperative agents, vastly outperforming

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.020
them.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:45.640
This is a fantastic paper, make sure to check
it out in the video description for more details.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:51.360
I found it to be very readable, so do not
despair if your math kung fu is not that strong.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.110
Just dive into it!

